1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,630
Aperture Desk Job puts a control panel
and button prompts inside the scene.

2
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:11,370
Half-Life: Alyx highlights objects
you can pick up as the hand moves.

3
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:15,790
Even with these cues, a press followed by
no visible change raises another question.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,550
Was the input missed, or received but unavailable in this state?

5
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:22,390
Let us try to open a door in an original test.

6
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:26,010
Both doors are locked, and both
panels receive the same button press.

7
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:29,605
One stays silent; the other briefly explains why the door is locked.

8
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:34,270
Acknowledging an input is different
from praising a successful action.

9
00:00:35,033 --> 00:00:36,953
Watch the small information panels that appear
as the pointer

10
00:00:36,953 --> 00:00:40,593
moves in this development demo of Oxygen Not
Included.

11
00:00:40,853 --> 00:00:43,633
Pointing at an object and reading its name and
status

12
00:00:43,633 --> 00:00:47,153
helps us understand what actions may be
available.

13
00:00:47,433 --> 00:00:49,813
This is a response for finding information,

14
00:00:50,013 --> 00:00:52,413
with a different role from a performance
grade.

15
00:00:53,013 --> 00:00:55,733
Selecting an object opens an information panel
on the right.

16
00:00:56,193 --> 00:00:58,913
Observe the changing tabs and contents within
that panel.

17
00:00:59,233 --> 00:01:03,173
This separates acknowledgement of selection
from progress on a real task.

18
00:01:03,173 --> 00:01:05,953
A separate later excerpt shows marked work
positions

19
00:01:05,953 --> 00:01:08,233
in the ground alongside moving characters.

20
00:01:08,633 --> 00:01:10,913
Reading pointer information, selection and
ongoing work

21
00:01:10,913 --> 00:01:15,213
separately can narrow down why an interaction
feels unresponsive.

22
00:01:15,733 --> 00:01:18,113
Ask the same questions in our locked-door
comparison:

23
00:01:18,593 --> 00:01:22,313
was the input received, and what state are we
in?

24
00:01:23,373 --> 00:01:26,643
This test lets us choose a spot for a building.

25
00:01:26,873 --> 00:01:31,423
Both panels refuse to build on a space that is already occupied.

26
00:01:31,713 --> 00:01:35,903
One changes color; the other also explains
that another building is in the way.

27
00:01:36,113 --> 00:01:40,023
On an empty space, insufficient
materials produce a different message.

28
00:01:40,273 --> 00:01:43,983
A different reason suggests a different next action.

29
00:01:44,173 --> 00:01:47,683
We avoid using one vague unavailable label for every condition.

30
00:01:47,873 --> 00:01:51,578
Keep the explanation brief and state
the reason in words as well as color.

31
00:01:51,593 --> 00:01:56,013
The quantities and layout in this
test are original teaching conditions.

32
00:01:56,733 --> 00:01:58,913
The food-production panel in Oxygen Not
Included

33
00:01:58,913 --> 00:02:01,193
shows an insufficient-resources status.

34
00:02:01,553 --> 00:02:03,313
Read the required materials and current state

35
00:02:03,313 --> 00:02:06,233
together instead of claiming a finished
product.

36
00:02:06,473 --> 00:02:09,913
This connects to explaining missing materials
in our

37
00:02:10,153 --> 00:02:12,493
build comparison: rejection can suggest a next
action.

38
00:02:12,753 --> 00:02:14,793
Material amounts and descriptions in the panel

39
00:02:14,793 --> 00:02:17,813
provide information about what needs to be
prepared.

40
00:02:17,993 --> 00:02:21,493
You can also inspect the material information
under the pointer in the following excerpt;

41
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:24,073
color need not explain every reason alone.

42
00:02:24,073 --> 00:02:28,033
We base the explanation on visible states
without

43
00:02:28,033 --> 00:02:30,993
adding an unseen product or success condition.

44
00:02:31,213 --> 00:02:36,073
In another excerpt, the contents change as
characters and work locations are inspected.

45
00:02:36,253 --> 00:02:39,713
Being able to inspect an object during ongoing
work

46
00:02:39,713 --> 00:02:42,633
helps us decide whether to wait or do
something else.

47
00:02:42,853 --> 00:02:47,093
In your game, separate the current obstacle
from the information to check next.

48
00:02:47,867 --> 00:02:51,697
A player might press a different confirmation key in the start menu.

49
00:02:51,947 --> 00:02:56,857
One panel accepts Enter; the other accepts Enter and Space.

50
00:02:57,087 --> 00:03:01,037
The second panel displays the keys it accepts beside the menu.

51
00:03:01,247 --> 00:03:04,917
This explores mapping familiar
confirmation keys to the same intention.

52
00:03:05,147 --> 00:03:09,737
Do not mix in buttons whose results differ, such as Back or Delete.

53
00:03:10,547 --> 00:03:13,817
Accepting more keys is not always the right choice.

54
00:03:13,967 --> 00:03:16,972
First check for conflicts with your game’s control conventions.

55
00:03:16,987 --> 00:03:21,547
Make the accepted input clear, then show the resulting state.

56
00:03:22,267 --> 00:03:25,067
The starting menu in Oxygen Not Included
changes

57
00:03:25,067 --> 00:03:28,107
the background of selected character cards.

58
00:03:28,327 --> 00:03:30,607
While reading abilities and traits,

59
00:03:30,607 --> 00:03:33,567
also observe which choices the menu currently
marks.

60
00:03:33,967 --> 00:03:37,807
Changing a choice and starting the game are
separate stages;

61
00:03:37,987 --> 00:03:40,887
the screen communicates a state before play
begins.

62
00:03:41,047 --> 00:03:45,207
The selected cards and traits can be inspected
before pressing the start button.

63
00:03:45,407 --> 00:03:48,367
This footage does not reveal keyboard inputs,

64
00:03:48,547 --> 00:03:51,647
so we do not infer support for both Enter and
Space.

65
00:03:51,647 --> 00:03:54,687
Our own comparison tested confirmation
conventions;

66
00:03:54,907 --> 00:03:58,347
this real menu lets us observe visible
selection state.

67
00:03:58,607 --> 00:04:02,087
The jobs menu shows another selection state
through a table and check marks.

68
00:04:02,287 --> 00:04:05,487
Observe the item under the pointer together
with its explanation.

69
00:04:05,747 --> 00:04:07,127
When the meaning of a menu changes,

70
00:04:07,127 --> 00:04:09,507
the information needed to confirm an action
changes too.

71
00:04:10,433 --> 00:04:14,783
A press intended to skip a cutscene can also seem to do nothing.

72
00:04:15,073 --> 00:04:18,923
In this test, a short press reveals the skip prompt.

73
00:04:19,093 --> 00:04:23,483
Holding the button fills an indicator;
only a full hold skips the scene.

74
00:04:23,813 --> 00:04:28,063
This choice protects against losing the
entire scene after one accidental tap.

75
00:04:28,333 --> 00:04:33,203
The game acknowledges the intention before asking for commitment.

76
00:04:33,373 --> 00:04:37,103
Release the button early to cancel
confirmation while the scene continues.

77
00:04:37,273 --> 00:04:40,418
A hold is not required for every cutscene.

78
00:04:40,433 --> 00:04:45,223
Distinguish an apparently missed press
from a press that requires a wait.

79
00:04:46,033 --> 00:04:48,453
This window selects a new character.

80
00:04:48,673 --> 00:04:51,773
Pressing Print closes it and adds a character.

81
00:04:52,093 --> 00:04:55,093
Separate a selection marker from the change
after confirmation.

82
00:04:55,373 --> 00:04:57,603
A different starting excerpt shows three
characters

83
00:04:57,673 --> 00:05:00,153
appearing in the game world after the menu.

84
00:05:00,473 --> 00:05:03,353
It is a different moment from adding the new
character;

85
00:05:03,713 --> 00:05:07,033
we do not present these cuts as one continuous
input.

86
00:05:07,293 --> 00:05:09,093
These examples separate what was being
selected

87
00:05:09,093 --> 00:05:12,973
from the state reached after confirmation.

88
00:05:12,973 --> 00:05:17,913
Next, watch actual play in which characters
work and building plans appear.

89
00:05:18,393 --> 00:05:20,993
Distinguishing selection from a change after
execution avoids

90
00:05:21,333 --> 00:05:24,513
announcing completion merely because an input
was acknowledged.

91
00:05:24,993 --> 00:05:28,713
The earlier cutscene comparison uses the same
criterion:

92
00:05:28,713 --> 00:05:30,993
show intent separately from committing the
actual action.

93
00:05:31,883 --> 00:05:35,353
Some actions, such as saving, do not finish immediately.

94
00:05:35,763 --> 00:05:39,913
This test first displays Saving when it receives a request.

95
00:05:40,163 --> 00:05:43,453
It does not say Saved while the operation is still pending.

96
00:05:43,643 --> 00:05:48,573
Another press during processing reports
progress instead of queuing more requests.

97
00:05:48,863 --> 00:05:51,773
The label changes only after completion is confirmed.

98
00:05:52,103 --> 00:05:56,513
A game with real persistence also
needs a response when saving fails.

99
00:05:56,823 --> 00:06:00,048
Here, a simulated delayed operation
illustrates the state transitions.

100
00:06:00,063 --> 00:06:05,233
Received, pending, and complete explain what the wait means.

101
00:06:05,950 --> 00:06:12,230
Work markers and character movement in Oxygen
Not Included communicate different states.

102
00:06:12,590 --> 00:06:16,570
A marked location does not prove completed
work;

103
00:06:16,890 --> 00:06:19,210
also watch what the characters actually do.

104
00:06:19,570 --> 00:06:24,790
As in the simulated save comparison, separate
receipt of a request from processing it.

105
00:06:25,010 --> 00:06:27,350
White building silhouettes in another excerpt
can

106
00:06:27,350 --> 00:06:29,290
also be distinguished from finished buildings.

107
00:06:29,570 --> 00:06:32,930
Inspect the silhouette, nearby work and
selected

108
00:06:32,930 --> 00:06:35,930
material information to separate planning from
progress.

109
00:06:35,950 --> 00:06:41,110
We do not invent an unseen Waiting message and
present it as a feature;

110
00:06:41,310 --> 00:06:43,670
we use observed shapes and actions.

111
00:06:43,950 --> 00:06:46,150
In the following excerpt, characters move and

112
00:06:46,150 --> 00:06:49,150
dig as the appearance of work locations
changes.

113
00:06:49,390 --> 00:06:51,290
The work markers remain while characters and
nearby terrain change;

114
00:06:51,290 --> 00:06:56,710
reading them together helps distinguish a plan
from work in progress.

115
00:06:56,830 --> 00:07:00,790
This does not verify a save function; the
visible state changes can inform

116
00:07:00,790 --> 00:07:05,050
how your own game distinguishes receipt,
progress and completion.

117
00:07:05,813 --> 00:07:10,043
A message and sound for every key can quickly clutter the screen.

118
00:07:10,313 --> 00:07:14,863
This test responds to interaction during
play and to confirmation in a menu.

119
00:07:15,093 --> 00:07:18,983
It does not attach a large warning to
keys with no meaning in the current state.

120
00:07:19,053 --> 00:07:23,743
Prioritize reasons when an action that appears available is blocked.

121
00:07:23,913 --> 00:07:28,823
Check whether the input arrived, why the
action is unavailable, and what to try next.

122
00:07:29,073 --> 00:07:32,418
A response does not need to make every attempted action succeed.

123
00:07:32,433 --> 00:07:35,983
It should make success, refusal, and waiting understandable.

124
00:07:36,553 --> 00:07:40,083
Start with moments in your game where
a player feels that nothing happened.

125
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:42,280
Turning on the gas overlay in Oxygen Not
Included

126
00:07:42,280 --> 00:07:45,620
changes the screen colors and the legend on
the right.

127
00:07:45,980 --> 00:07:49,420
Even in the same location, useful pointer

128
00:07:49,420 --> 00:07:52,560
information depends on the current viewing
context.

129
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:55,800
This suggests communicating the selected tool
and

130
00:07:55,800 --> 00:07:58,840
state instead of repeating one message for
every input.

131
00:07:59,060 --> 00:08:01,880
The red X in the next excerpt is the Cancel
Tool;

132
00:08:02,140 --> 00:08:05,060
red alone does not mean a failed building
placement.

133
00:08:05,300 --> 00:08:10,180
Observe actual guidance while changing tools
or pointing at a different object.

134
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:16,340
We do not infer a response to every key; this
example distinguishes visible tool roles.

135
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:19,820
In a later work excerpt, read material
information,

136
00:08:19,820 --> 00:08:22,000
character actions and building plans
separately.

137
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:25,900
The needed response depends on what was
selected,

138
00:08:25,900 --> 00:08:28,760
what is pending and what changed, even within
one screen.

139
00:08:28,940 --> 00:08:31,680
Connect the earlier explanations to actual
moments in your game: replace

140
00:08:31,880 --> 00:08:36,180
seemingly unresponsive inputs with responses
that reveal the state and reason.
